  "slug": "ctcf-specific-8112",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test CTCF target recognition in yeast and transcription-driven boundary formation, extending insulation models beyond cohesin.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how CTCF-associated architecture and chromatin boundaries are established. A naive-genome study expressing human CTCF in budding yeast reports that it localizes to chromatin but binds specific sites weakly and does not robustly drive transcription, suggesting target recognition depends on evolutionary context. A second preprint shows that transcription activation at the Fbn2 locus forms cohesin-independent decompaction and insulation, while transcriptional bursts disrupt CTCF looping, analyzed by Micro-C. AMBER maps accessible-chromatin associations into multi-scale regulatory hubs in mouse brain, and a yeast study addresses how intrinsically disordered regions modulate DNA binding by Msn2, Msn4 and Com2. These items are heterogeneous; the new element is that insulation can arise from transcription independently of cohesin-driven barrier formation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
